version: 2
title: How a version-1 data works
description: Data rows, one JSON object per line, shown as a paged, searchable, sortable table with the columns found — or, with a directive line, a live view composed from other files through a table policy.
decisions:
  - key: form
    label: What is in the file?
    values:
      - key: raw
        label: Rows
        detail: JSON objects, one per line (or one JSON array of them). The table is the file.
      - key: composed
        label: A directive line
        detail: "A line whose keys start with `$` — `$sources`, `$policy`, `$title`, `$description`, `$labels` — makes the file a live view over other files."
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of a line, what a directive is, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shape of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shape of the file
        description: Not YAML — JSON Lines, with directive lines for a composed view.
        sections:
          - title: A row
            body: |
              One JSON object per line; the columns are the union of the objects' keys in the
              order first seen. A file whose whole text is one JSON array of objects is accepted
              too. Values render as text — strings as they are, numbers and booleans as written,
              null and missing empty, objects and arrays as compact JSON — and that text is what
              the search and the sort see. Nothing is authored in the view: a `.jsonl` is written
              by whatever produced the data.
          - title: Directive lines
            body: |
              ```json
              {"$sources": ["accommodation.json#properties", "more.jsonl", "fixes.middleware"], "$policy": "cheapest.policy"}
              {"$title": "Stays by price", "$description": "Thu 1 - Mon 5 Oct, 4 nights, two adults"}
              {"$labels": {"best_offer.price_total_aud": "Total (4 nights, AUD)"}}
              ```

              | directive | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `$sources` | the files the rows are read from on every open: a `.json` (`#key` naming its list, else its longest list of objects), a `.jsonl` (its raw lines), a `.csv` or `.tsv` (first row the header), or a `.middleware` (its source, amended) — resolved against this file's folder |
              | `$policy` | the table policy (`role: table`) the rows pass through: kept, ordered, cut to its columns |
              | `$title`, `$description` | the view's own words |
              | `$labels` | header names by field, dot paths included |

              Directive lines may be split; `$sources` and `$labels` accumulate, a second scalar
              replaces the first with a note. Raw lines beside a directive are added to the
              composed rows. Either key alone works: sources without a policy is the union of the
              files; a policy without sources applies to the raw lines. Nothing is cached — the
              file holds the references, never the result, so it can never drift from its data;
              what is said ABOUT the data lives here, the policy names rules only.
          - title: Where the engine lives
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/dataRows.ts` — `parseDataRows`, `parseSourceRef`,
              `rowsOfSource`, `cellText`, `valueAt`, `searchIndex`, `matchingRows`,
              `compareValues`, `sortRows`, `pageOf`; its header comment is what `studio-check
              --spec jsonl` prints, with `tablePolicy.ts` beside it. The chain reader is
              `dataSources.ts` (`readSourceRows`, `chainText`). The view is
              `components/data/DataTableView.tsx` (`DataGrid`, the composed view, the Collect
              button) with `RowDialog.tsx` and `cells.tsx`; the checker is `python/studio_kinds/kinds/jsonl.py`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: parseDataRows reads every line and never throws; the grid is drawn — or the sources are read first.
    hint: Say what is in the file — rows draw at once; a directive line reads other files first.
    content:
      key: draw
      label: What is read, and what is drawn
      kind: brief
      by: [form]
      docs:
        form=raw:
          title: What is read, and what is drawn
          description: The lines, then the grid.
          sections:
            - title: The parse
              body: |
                Blank lines are skipped. A line that is not JSON, or not an object, is a problem
                named by its line — `line 12: Unexpected token…`, `line 12: not a JSON object` —
                and the row is skipped. A text starting with `[` is tried as one array first. The
                columns are gathered from the rows kept.
            - title: The grid
              body: |
                A strip: `N rows · N columns`, and `N match` while a search is on; "Search every
                field" (every word must match, case-insensitive, applied 150 ms after typing,
                Escape clears); rows per page (25, 50, 100, 250; 50 to start) and a pager
                `from–to of total`. Problems under it: `N lines skipped: line 12: …`. Then the
                table: a `#` column with the row's original number, one column per key, headers
                sortable, cells in monospace truncated at 360px with the whole text on hover; a
                cell that is a link opens in the browser and has a copy button beside it.
            - title: Empty
              body: |
                No rows and problems: "No rows could be read — one JSON object per line is the
                shape." with up to eight of them and the text underneath. No rows and no problems:
                "No rows — one JSON object per line." The file's text shows in either case.
        form=composed:
          title: What is read, and what is drawn
          description: The directive, the sources in order, the policy, then the grid with a lead.
          sections:
            - title: The directive
              body: |
                A `$` line is not a row. Named problems: `a directive line carries no row fields —
                "k" ignored`, `unknown directive "$x" (known: …)`, `$policy must be a file ref`,
                `$title must be text`, `a second $policy — this one applies`, `$labels must be an
                object of field: label`, `$labels."f" must be text`, `$sources must be a list of
                files`, `$sources entry N is not a file ref`.
            - title: Reading the sources
              body: |
                "Reading a.json, b.jsonl…" while `readSourceRows` reads each source in order,
                resolved against this file's folder. A `.csv`/`.tsv` takes its first row as the
                header ("the csv has no header row" otherwise). A `.json` object gives the list at
                `#key` (`"key" is not a list in x.json`; `x.json#key: some entries are not objects`)
                or its longest list of objects (`x.json holds no list of objects — name one with
                #key`). A `.jsonl` gives its raw lines — a source that composes rows itself is
                not followed: `x.jsonl composes rows itself — only its raw lines were read`. A
                `.middleware` is followed to its own source, up to eight deep, a cycle refused
                (`a cycle — it is already being read (a → b)`), and its rules applied on the way
                back. An unreadable file: `could not read x`. The raw lines of this file are
                appended after every source.
            - title: The policy
              body: |
                `$policy` is read from beside this file and must be `role: table` — otherwise
                `x.policy is not a table policy (role: y) — the rows show as they are`; unreadable,
                `could not read x.policy`. `applyTablePolicy` keeps the rows every `where` clause
                holds for (dot paths reach into nested objects; text ops case-insensitive; numeric
                ops never true for an empty field), sorts by the keys in order (ties keep the
                source order), cuts to `limit`, and picks the columns: the listed ones in order,
                `"*"` standing for every other column after them, `hide` subtracted; without
                `columns`, every column the rows carry. Then it names `column "x": no row carries
                it` and `sort "x": no row carries it`.
            - title: The grid and its lead
              body: |
                The same grid, headed by a lead: the `$title` (else the file's name) · `N rows` or
                `N of M rows` · `from <chain> (N rows, N amended), …` · `+ N raw lines` · `through
                x.policy` · the rules in one line (`2 filters · sorted by price asc, then km asc ·
                first 100`, in the view's labels) · the Collect button. Headers show `$labels`.
                Every problem — the directive's, the sources', the policy's — is in the strip.
  - key: searched
    label: The rows are searched, sorted or paged
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: Session-only narrowing; a row click opens the row whole.
    content:
      key: grid
      label: What the grid does
      kind: md
      doc: |
        A search keeps the rows whose text — every column's, joined — contains every word.
        A header click sorts ascending, a second descending, a third clears; numbers sort before
        text when both occur, text by locale with numbers inside it read as numbers, empties last
        whichever the direction, ties in original order. A new search, sort, page size or set of
        rows goes back to page one and closes the dialog. Clicking a row opens it in full — every
        column and its value, with previous and next over the rows in their current order and the
        row as JSON to copy. Row numbers are the rows' original positions. Nothing is written.
  - key: chain-changed
    label: A file in the chain changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    when: [form=composed]
    detail: Any file read — a source, a middleware behind it, the policy — fires studio:fs-changed and the view reads again.
    content:
      key: rewatch
      label: What is re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The composed view remembers every file it read — the sources, each middleware in a chain
        and the raw file under it, the policy — and listens for `studio:fs-changed` on any of
        them: a scrape re-run, a correction added, a rule changed, and the rows are read again and
        the policy re-applied. This file's own change (the desktop watcher, the web folder's
        events, VS Code's text document) re-parses the directive and reads again too. The search,
        the sort and the page are state and stay.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    when: [form=raw]
    detail: The host re-reads; the grid re-parses and goes back to page one.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: The re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text, and the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error. The rows and columns are re-parsed; a new set of
        rows returns the grid to page one and closes the row dialog; the search and the sort
        stay.
  - key: collected
    label: The rows are collected
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Elsewhere
    detail: The rows the view shows are copied into a .collection beside it, to decide over one at a time.
    content:
      key: collect
      label: What Collect writes
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The Collect button sits in a composed view's lead when the host has a writer. It asks
        for a name (`<stem>.collection`, checked against the folder's files) and writes, beside
        this file, a collection whose `source` is this file's name, whose `fields` are the
        columns shown, whose `labels` are the `$labels`, whose `stats` are the numeric fields and
        the first link, and whose items are the rows shown with ids 1, 2, 3… in order — then opens
        it. `studio-check --collect x.jsonl [name.collection]` writes the same from the command
        line, for a raw file too.
  - key: read-elsewhere
    label: Another file reads it
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Elsewhere
    detail: A .jsonl is a source to other views, to a middleware, and to a collection.
    content:
      key: elsewhere
      label: Who reads a data file
      kind: md
      doc: |
        - Another `.jsonl` names it in `$sources` and takes its raw lines (never its directive).
        - A `.middleware` names it as `source` and amends its rows on the way to a view.
        - A `.collection` records it as `source` — the file the items were copied from.
        - A table `.policy` never names it; the policy is named from here.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — on the command line a composed view is read the way the viewer reads it.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: Not YAML, so the engine's own problems are the check.
        sections:
          - title: Rows
            body: |
              `parseDataRows`: every skipped line is a problem. Summary `N rows × N columns`, on the
              command line and the page alike.
          - title: A composed view, on the command line
            body: |
              The sources are read from the disk beside the file — chains followed, middlewares
              applied — and the policy read and applied; every problem the viewer would show is
              reported, plus `policy x: not found beside this file` and `policy x: role is y, not
              table`. Summary: `composed: N of M rows from <chain> (N rows), … + N raw through
              x.policy`.
          - title: A composed view, on the page
            body: |
              The directive is parsed and nothing is read: summary `N rows × N columns + N
              sources`, and a note `composed from files not checked — this check has no folder:
              a.json, x.policy`.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              That the columns are consistent across rows. That a `$labels` field exists. That a
              source's rows carry what the policy sorts by (the viewer says so).
  - key: authored
    label: A data file is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a data file
        description: Rows from a producer, or a view composed from files that already exist.
        decisions:
          - key: want
            label: What do you want?
            values:
              - { key: rows, label: A table of rows }
              - { key: view, label: A live view over other files }
        steps:
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template jsonl > new.jsonl` — two rows:

              ```json
              {"id":1,"name":"First row","amount":10,"done":true}
              {"id":2,"name":"Second row","amount":20,"done":false,"note":"any key becomes a column"}
              ```
          - key: rows
            label: Write or produce the rows
            after: [template]
            when: [want=rows]
            detail: |
              One object per line, no trailing commas, no blank objects. Keep keys the same across
              rows; a key that appears once is a mostly-empty column.
          - key: sources
            label: Name the sources
            after: [template]
            when: [want=view]
            detail: |
              Replace the rows with one line: `{"$sources": ["data.json#items"], "$title": "…"}`.
              Name a `.middleware` instead of the raw file when corrections exist.
          - key: policy
            label: Add the table policy
            after: [sources]
            when: [want=view]
            detail: |
              Write `x.policy` with `role: table` beside the file — `where`, `sort`, `columns`,
              `limit` — and add `"$policy": "x.policy"` to the directive. Header names go in
              `$labels` here, never in the policy.
          - key: check
            label: Check it
            after: [rows, policy]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.jsonl` — every skipped line is named; a composed view is read from
              disk and its policy's problems reported. Then open it and search.
